		<div class="table-wrap">
			<p>This is a list of games I have played / remember playing.</p>
			<p>There are <b>REPLACE_GAME_COUNT</b> games on this list.</p>
	            <p>If you are interested in the other things I do, they are on <a href="https://jessekaukonen.net" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">my main website</a>.</p>
			<p>The finished date is often an approximate for older games that I added long after the fact.</p>
			<p>Games I consider some of the best games of all time are marked in <span class="gold">gold</span>.</p>
			<h3>My approach to reviewing games</h3>
			<p>Reviews contain spoilers by default. Some reviews start by stating they are spoiler-free. This is mostly reserved for games with cool twists that should never be spoiled.</p>
			<p>I separate subjective and objective opinions. For example, I might say I don't like this art style, and applaud the developers for pulling off that art style in such a masterful fashion. Or I might dislike the genre, and still consider it to be the best game in that genre ever made (Doom 2016). The idea is to help a person decide if they want to play a game or not, and whether to buy it at full price or at a heavy discount.</p> 
			<p>Some games are completed, others I stopped playing after deciding I had seen enough. I don't care about achievements, and wish I could just disable the popups.</p>
			<p>I don't care about multiplayer at all, so realtime strategy games only consider the singleplayer campaign, and Dark Souls style games offline mode only.</p>
			<p>I play all games blind without looking up anything except in extreme circumstances. Sometimes this means I take over a year to complete a game, take breaks, and restart with a fresh save file to get a new perspective on the problems I couldn't solve earlier.</p>
			<p>When it comes to <b>what I look for in games</b>, the common patterns are: World that feels alive without needing the player as a vehicle of agency (The Last Express), ability to experience the story and the world in many ways (Hollow Knight), no handholding (Dark Souls), no notifications or other interruptions to being immersed in the experience (Thief), and letting the player observe and understand (Outer Wilds). While the art style matters a lot to me, graphical fidely does not. I consider ambiance and atmosphere more important than story.</p>
			<p>If you wish to recommend me games, please email me at jesse.kaukonen@gmail.com. I'm a patient gamer, so don't recommend me new releases.</p>
